adr: 0215
file: selfhost/apfront.pas
kills: slice_escape
why: AP 6.4.9's containment is by construction: a bare name is put back in tqAt/tqLen, so a program of ISO/IEC 10206:1991 takes 6.4.9's own path and every rule the clause already had is asked the question it was written for. Sending a bare name down the access path instead looks harmless -- InquiryOfAccess answers the same type for `type of n` -- and it is not: ResolveInquiry's two refusals are written against the bare path and stop being asked. The slice refusal is the reachable one, and AP 6.7.3.9.2 is the reason it matters: `type of a` over a slice formal is the only denoter that can produce a slice type without writing `array of`, so losing the refusal admits a slice type into a type-definition and a variable, each holding a descriptor nothing filled in (ADR-0143). The schematic-formal refusal goes the same way. Re-anchored twice after ADR-0232. Its killer was `dialect-containment`, which compiled the conformance corpus a second way and is retired; `typeinquiry_access_errors` was tried and does *not* kill it, because InquiryOfAccess asks OtherListsParameter as well -- so the closest-containing rule was never one of the two this loses, which the first version of this file said it was.
--- old
          if obj^.kind = nkVar then begin
            t^.tqAt := obj^.vrAt;
            t^.tqLen := obj^.vrLen
          end
          else
            t^.tqObj := obj
--- new
          t^.tqObj := obj
